  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1225 PM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nRest of Today - Monday Morning: Areas of Frost Tonight\n\nRather benign weather is expected through the remainder of the \nafternoon though some northwest wind gusts of 20-25mph are possible \nacross the Upper Mississippi River Valley, though favored across \nportions of north-central to central Wisconsin given a well mixed \nboundary layer and 925-850hPa winds of 25-30mph.\n\nHeading into tonight, winds begin to diminish as low level ridging \nand high pressure move into the region. Clear to mostly clear skies \nare also expected which should allow for fairly efficient cooling \nacross the Upper Mississippi River Valley. This cooling combined \nwith the light winds and mostly clear skies leads to a favorable set-\nup for frost development as temperatures approach and fall below the \n37 degree mark, especially over the sandier areas of north-central \nto central Wisconsin where the 04.13z NBM suggests a 10-20% chance \nfor temperatures to approach freezing. Have maintained the Frost \nAdvisory due to this potential. \n\n\nMonday - Friday: Mostly Dry, Near to Slightly Above Normal \nTemperatures. \n\nDry conditions are expected throughout the week with the only \nexception being Monday night into Tuesday morning as a 700hPa \nshortwave trough moves eastward across the Upper Midwest. While the \nmajority of the region is also expected to remain dry during this \nperiod, probabilities for measurable rainfall have increased \nfor those north of I-94. The 04.00z LREF does have some higher \nprobabilities compared to other ensemble guidance, sitting \naround 10-30% compared the the NBM at ~10%. Thinking the reason\nfor this discrepancy is the LREF is largely inflated by its EPS\nmembership which suggests deeper moisture present across the \nregion, resulting in probabilities nearing 50% whereas other \nsolutions such as the GEFS/GEPS are on the drier side and \nsuggest little to no potential in our area, keeping rainfall to \nthe north. Tending towards the NBM solution for the time being, \nbut if the shortwave trends southward or the GEFS/GEPS solutions\ntrend towards increased moisture, probabilities for rainfall \nwill likely increase. \n\nOtherwise, little in the way of airmass change is expected \nthroughout the week under northwest flow which results in fairly \nstagnant, near to just above normal temperatures for this time of \nyear. The exception also comes Tuesday when high temperatures are \nfavored to climb into the low 70s behind a warm front that lifts \nnorth as the aforementioned wave moves through the region and low \nlevel thermal ridging builds in, but fall back towards normal \nThursday behind a cold front.",
